[17592186178987 #edb/ref 1080 "12%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "122f2591-2572-58fe-ad18-214dfc688121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178988 #edb/ref 1080 "16%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178988 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1cc9d0c7-9fe5-5e91-a6bc-d8a400aa2938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178989 #edb/ref 1080 "8%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7536fd18-fd67-5468-8f24-8ec13351d149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179496 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179496 #edb/ref 1071 "Winston drove $200$ kilometers in two days. If he drove $110$ kilometers on the first day, what percentage of the total distance did he drive on the first day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75ac9866-6b0f-5736-a592-0bade16b8814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179496 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the part is the number of kilometers Winston drove the first day, which is $110$, and\n- the whole is the total number of kilometers he drove, which is $200$.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 110 \\div 200 = \\dfrac{110}{200}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{200} = \\dfrac{110 \\div 2}{200 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{55}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{55}{100} = 55 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Winston drove $55 \\%$ of the total distance on the first day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179497 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179497 #edb/ref 1071 "In his previous youth baseball season, Matt hit $75$ extra-bases, $9$ of which were home runs. What percentage of extra-base hits were home runs?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f3270cc-d191-5282-b9f7-cf63dcd69d70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179497 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the part is the number of home runs, which is $9$, and\n- the whole is the total number of extra-base hits Matt connected, which is $75$.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 9 \\div 75 = \\dfrac{9}{75}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{75}=\\dfrac{9 \\div 3}{75 \\div 3}=\\dfrac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{25} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 4}{25 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{12}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{100} = 12 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12 \\%$ of extra-base hits were home runs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179819 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179819 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179819 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179819 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179819 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179820 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179820 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179820 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding a Percentage Given Two Numbers: Word Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179897 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "39af6225-7719-5f09-ae8c-6df89ff6b7cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180240 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180240 #edb/ref 1071 "In a supermarket, $16$ out of $200$ watermelons were unripe. What percentage of watermelons were unripe?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e688fddb-8c8a-5d00-8ff1-44d42499d959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1061 7964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bdf65be2-4038-5c62-b8e0-2362d10939cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1061 7965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a3797e0-441d-5d87-adba-4fa2a33a82b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180640 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180641 #edb/ref 1061 7966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0c640ea-bd66-5449-a695-d9913915d65f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1064 "If we start with the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nwe can use the relationship between multiplication and division to write down a formula to calculate a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how to apply this formula using a real-world example.\n\nSuppose that $54$ out of $300$ boxes of grapes in a supermarket contain red grapes. What percentage of the boxes contain red grapes?\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of red grapes, which is $54$, and the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $300$.\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 54 \\div 300 = \\dfrac{54}{300}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{54}{300} = \\dfrac{54 \\div 3}{300 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{18}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{100} = 18 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\%$ of the boxes contain red grapes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1065 2082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "73bca8be-ae46-5214-9ba6-2beb553be49f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180661 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
